00:59:39.870 --> 00:59:40.890
What's the status?

1388
00:59:41.190 --> 00:59:46.110
Where are they in their search for this very hard to find potato chip bag?

1389
00:59:46.730 --> 00:59:48.450
That's actually in the vending machine.

1390
00:59:48.490 --> 00:59:49.910
That might be in the vending machine.

1391
00:59:50.290 --> 00:59:51.910
That is in the vending machine.

1392
00:59:52.170 --> 00:59:53.010
But go ahead.

1393
00:59:53.570 --> 00:59:57.050
That, according to a fan catch, is B1 in the vending machine.

1394
00:59:57.050 --> 00:59:59.050
Yeah, it's right there waiting for them.

1395
00:59:59.690 --> 01:00:06.010
Well, Karen kind of impresses Jim because she decides to call Montreal, the Montreal

1396
01:00:06.010 --> 01:00:07.710
factory, to see if they have them.

1397
01:00:07.810 --> 01:00:09.350
And she speaks French.

1398
01:00:10.070 --> 01:00:10.510
May we.

1399
01:00:10.690 --> 01:00:11.430
May we.

1400
01:00:11.570 --> 01:00:12.110
May we.

1401
01:00:12.210 --> 01:00:14.230
A lot of people wrote in about this.

1402
01:00:14.610 --> 01:00:19.850
Brianna Bullard and many, many others want to know, does Rashida Jones actually know French?

1403
01:00:20.490 --> 01:00:25.150
So I texted Rashida, since we're already texting about her breakfast order.

1404
01:00:25.350 --> 01:00:28.010
She's probably like, Angela, what is happening?

1405
01:00:29.310 --> 01:00:30.850
This is what she said.

1406
01:00:30.970 --> 01:00:34.550
She goes, no, at this time, I didn't know French.

1407
01:00:34.550 --> 01:00:37.430
I worked really hard on that line.

1408
01:00:37.450 --> 01:00:43.810
But a few years later, I ended up going to France to do a French immersion school in the

1409
01:00:43.810 --> 01:00:44.590
south of France.

1410
01:00:44.590 --> 01:00:48.910
And maybe this scene was the beginning, and it got me inspired.

1411
01:00:50.170 --> 01:00:50.650
Wow.

1412
01:00:51.290 --> 01:00:52.130
That's crazy.

1413
01:00:52.130 --> 01:00:54.770
She did not speak French, but now she does.

1414
01:00:55.070 --> 01:00:55.430
Yes.

1415
01:00:55.530 --> 01:00:59.050
I was like, I want to go to a French immersion class in the south of France.

1416
01:00:59.050 --> 01:01:00.330
That sounds amazing.

1417
01:01:01.230 --> 01:01:01.710
Same.

1418
01:01:02.650 --> 01:01:03.130
Same.

1419
01:01:03.130 --> 01:01:04.450
I took French in high school.

1420
01:01:04.890 --> 01:01:05.510
You did?

1421
01:01:05.710 --> 01:01:08.010
Oh, I have a story about that, actually.

1422
01:01:08.430 --> 01:01:08.610
Yeah.

1423
01:01:08.610 --> 01:01:10.030
Can I tell this real quickly?

1424
01:01:10.310 --> 01:01:10.550
Yeah.

1425
01:01:10.770 --> 01:01:12.890
I took French in high school from Sister Landry.

1426
01:01:12.890 --> 01:01:14.230
I went to a Catholic school.

1427
01:01:14.410 --> 01:01:16.190
Sister Landry was my French teacher.

1428
01:01:16.990 --> 01:01:21.450
And I dreamed, I told you guys this before, I dreamed from the time I was a little girl

1429
01:01:21.450 --> 01:01:22.850
that I would one day go to Paris.

1430
01:01:23.150 --> 01:01:25.430
And then in my 30s, it finally happened.

1431
01:01:25.590 --> 01:01:28.750
Of course, at this point, I was now very far from my French training.

1432
01:01:30.090 --> 01:01:31.410
Couldn't really speak French.

1433
01:01:31.810 --> 01:01:34.370
But Lee and I, we went to a little farmer's market.

1434
01:01:34.530 --> 01:01:37.990
And I guess in France, you don't touch the fruit.

1435
01:01:38.250 --> 01:01:38.750
Uh-oh.

1436
01:01:38.890 --> 01:01:39.150
Okay?

1437
01:01:39.490 --> 01:01:42.250
You don't select your own produce.

1438
01:01:42.710 --> 01:01:43.970
You point to it.

1439
01:01:44.090 --> 01:01:48.350
Or you trust the person selling the produce to select the best pieces for you.

1440
01:01:49.470 --> 01:01:50.830
I didn't know this.

1441
01:01:50.950 --> 01:01:51.190
Okay?

1442
01:01:51.550 --> 01:01:52.490
Oh, my gosh.

1443
01:01:52.710 --> 01:01:53.190
I would have been.

1444
01:01:53.290 --> 01:01:54.110
I pick up things.

1445
01:01:54.110 --> 01:01:55.130
I thump them.

1446
01:01:55.230 --> 01:01:56.390
I smell them.

1447
01:01:56.730 --> 01:01:57.550
No, lady.

1448
01:01:57.730 --> 01:01:57.910
Uh-oh.

1449
01:01:57.910 --> 01:01:58.530
Don't do it.

1450
01:01:58.590 --> 01:01:58.850
Uh-oh.

1451
01:01:58.850 --> 01:02:02.250
If you go to a farmer's market in France, here's my hot tip for you.

1452
01:02:02.330 --> 01:02:03.430
Don't thump the melon.

1453
01:02:03.790 --> 01:02:04.810
Don't thump the melon.

1454
01:02:04.810 --> 01:02:06.150
Don't thump the melon in France.

1455
01:02:06.210 --> 01:02:06.650
Okay?

1456
01:02:06.810 --> 01:02:07.070
Okay.

1457
01:02:07.070 --> 01:02:12.450
Now, all through this trip, we'd been going to restaurants, and we'd been going to tourist

1458
01:02:12.450 --> 01:02:15.130
places, and I was just fumbling with my French.

1459
01:02:15.130 --> 01:02:16.430
I couldn't get anything out.

1460
01:02:16.630 --> 01:02:19.770
I could read the menus, but I couldn't order anything.

1461
01:02:19.770 --> 01:02:23.610
I thought I was going to really help us, but I couldn't help us.

1462
01:02:23.690 --> 01:02:24.570
But here we are.

1463
01:02:24.670 --> 01:02:25.930
We're at this little market.

1464
01:02:26.130 --> 01:02:27.850
I have done the faux pas.

1465
01:02:28.090 --> 01:02:29.110
I believe that's French.

1466
01:02:29.210 --> 01:02:31.450
The faux pas of touching the fruit.

1467
01:02:31.930 --> 01:02:37.890
And this woman turns to her co-worker and goes off on me.

1468
01:02:37.890 --> 01:02:44.050
She, in French, is calling me, like, kind of like a dumb American.

1469
01:02:44.430 --> 01:02:44.830
You're a piece of trash.

1470
01:02:44.830 --> 01:02:50.150
Yeah, basically, whatever it was, just these Americans come here, and they touch her fruit.

1471
01:02:50.290 --> 01:02:52.270
She was, like, going on and on, okay?

1472
01:02:52.270 --> 01:02:53.210
In French.

1473
01:02:54.970 --> 01:03:01.430
I was this mixture of just, like, embarrassed, but also felt like, I'm sorry.

1474
01:03:01.610 --> 01:03:03.650
How was I supposed to know that?

1475
01:03:03.990 --> 01:03:05.950
I'm a nice person.

1476
01:03:07.530 --> 01:03:09.070
I'm not a bad person.

1477
01:03:09.070 --> 01:03:10.990
I'm sorry I picked up that banana.

1478
01:03:12.030 --> 01:03:18.270
And Angela, this fluent French, spilled out of my mouth at this woman.

1479
01:03:18.450 --> 01:03:22.070
And in French, I suddenly had the command to say,

1480
01:03:22.210 --> 01:03:26.430
I'm so sorry, but in the United States, we pick our own fruit.

1481
01:03:26.430 --> 01:03:27.930
I didn't know about your custom.

1482
01:03:27.930 --> 01:03:31.770
I apologize, but you don't need to say nasty things about me.

1483
01:03:32.210 --> 01:03:34.610
And the look on her face was like, oh!

1484
01:03:34.610 --> 01:03:40.170
Because she certainly didn't think that the fruit-touching American could speak French or

1485
01:03:40.170 --> 01:03:41.110
understand French.

1486
01:03:41.230 --> 01:03:45.710
And by the way, I didn't know that I had such a command of the language until I was insulted

1487
01:03:45.710 --> 01:03:47.910
in a farmer's market in Paris.

1488
01:03:48.110 --> 01:03:49.630
And then your superpower came out.

1489
01:03:50.170 --> 01:03:50.530
Yes.

1490
01:03:50.670 --> 01:03:55.090
You know how, like, some superheroes, their power doesn't come through until they're in

1491
01:03:55.090 --> 01:03:55.610
crisis.

1492
01:03:56.310 --> 01:03:59.310
I was like a mom lifting a car off her baby.

1493
01:03:59.310 --> 01:03:59.710
Exactly.

1494
01:04:00.090 --> 01:04:00.430
All right.

1495
01:04:00.650 --> 01:04:05.870
So now we're back at Dunder Mifflin, and Kelly is crying by the copier in the annex.

1496
01:04:06.310 --> 01:04:06.510
Yeah.

1497
01:04:06.730 --> 01:04:07.630
She is in tears.

1498
01:04:08.250 --> 01:04:13.950
Michael thinks he's finally made a breakthrough until she reveals that she's really crying

1499
01:04:13.950 --> 01:04:15.250
about her plans with Ryan.

1500
01:04:15.550 --> 01:04:15.690
Yeah.

1501
01:04:16.730 --> 01:04:18.670
I have a question about this scene.

1502
01:04:18.750 --> 01:04:18.910
Okay.

1503
01:04:19.910 --> 01:04:22.870
What is up with this second copier in the annex?

1504
01:04:23.130 --> 01:04:24.410
Is it always there?

1505
01:04:24.850 --> 01:04:26.430
I have never seen it before.

1506
01:04:27.050 --> 01:04:28.090
What is happening?

1507
01:04:28.270 --> 01:04:31.470
I think they just rolled it in from the bullpen for the scene.

1508
01:04:32.230 --> 01:04:36.830
I feel like they did, and they did it because in the background, in the break room, Dwight

1509
01:04:36.830 --> 01:04:37.630
is in there.

1510
01:04:37.770 --> 01:04:42.510
You can see him over Kelly's shoulder trying to shove this bird into an empty soda can.

1511
01:04:42.690 --> 01:04:47.110
I feel like that's why they had to put the copier there to combine these two moments, to

1512
01:04:47.110 --> 01:04:49.110
have a reason for Michael to be back there.

1513
01:04:49.250 --> 01:04:49.430
Right.

1514
01:04:49.490 --> 01:04:51.970
And for him to see over his shoulder and all of that.

1515
01:04:52.210 --> 01:04:52.610
Exactly.

1516
01:04:54.410 --> 01:04:56.690
In that hallway, they just shoved it there.

1517
01:04:56.930 --> 01:04:57.010
Yeah.

1518
01:04:57.330 --> 01:04:58.390
I did not think so.

1519
01:04:58.650 --> 01:05:00.430
I feel like I will track this.

1520
01:05:00.550 --> 01:05:03.950
It's going to be a soft track, but I am going to look for this copier more.

1521
01:05:04.090 --> 01:05:09.270
I think it should be a very soft track because I would be bold enough to say I think it might

1522
01:05:09.270 --> 01:05:10.990
be the only time it's in the annex.

1523
01:05:11.530 --> 01:05:17.770
I know that's setting myself up for some fan mail of Angela actually in season eight, but

1524
01:05:17.770 --> 01:05:21.990
I'm just saying I don't think that copier visits the annex very often.

1525
01:05:22.610 --> 01:05:26.910
Guys, if you're watching another episode and you see that copier, write to us.

1526
01:05:27.150 --> 01:05:32.570
You can go to our website, officeladies.com and submit questions or comments or write

1527
01:05:32.570 --> 01:05:34.970
to us at officeladies at earwolf.com.

1528
01:05:35.050 --> 01:05:35.610
Here we go.

1529
01:05:35.850 --> 01:05:36.310
We want to know.

1530
01:05:36.490 --> 01:05:37.190
We want to know.

1531
01:05:37.510 --> 01:05:37.690
All right.

1532
01:05:37.790 --> 01:05:42.570
But this leads us into one of my favorite Dwight talking heads where he explains he's

1533
01:05:42.570 --> 01:05:43.190
justifying.

1534
01:05:43.370 --> 01:05:45.430
Why is he shoving this bird into a can?

1535
01:05:45.430 --> 01:05:48.530
And it's because he grew up on a farm.

1536
01:05:49.390 --> 01:05:53.250
And guys, they reburied his grandfather in an old oil drum.

1537
01:05:55.250 --> 01:05:56.330
OK, hold on.

1538
01:05:56.450 --> 01:06:00.910
I mean, the same the same grandfather that was buried in the suit that he wore.

1539
01:06:01.110 --> 01:06:01.790
Like, what's happening?

1540
01:06:01.930 --> 01:06:04.130
How many times has this guy been brought up out of the ground?

1541
01:06:04.370 --> 01:06:06.390
This is one of my favorite things about our show.

1542
01:06:06.750 --> 01:06:07.430
Teresa S.

1543
01:06:07.630 --> 01:06:08.230
also loves it.

1544
01:06:08.370 --> 01:06:13.430
She wrote in to say in Casino Night, Dwight says that his grandfather was buried in the

1545
01:06:13.430 --> 01:06:14.510
tuxedo he's wearing.

1546
01:06:14.510 --> 01:06:14.870
Yeah.

1547
01:06:15.450 --> 01:06:19.630
And in grief counseling, he said his grandfather was reburied in an oil drum.

1548
01:06:19.810 --> 01:06:19.950
Yeah.

1549
01:06:20.650 --> 01:06:21.090
Reburied?

1550
01:06:21.250 --> 01:06:21.390
What?

1551
01:06:21.470 --> 01:06:21.830
She wrote.

1552
01:06:22.510 --> 01:06:22.950
Reburied.

1553
01:06:23.110 --> 01:06:23.450
What happened?

1554
01:06:23.930 --> 01:06:26.510
What is the story behind Dwight's grandfather?

1555
01:06:26.710 --> 01:06:28.250
Was he exhumed for some reason?

1556
01:06:28.610 --> 01:06:33.590
And did Dwight take the tuxedo off his dead grandfather before reburying him?

1557
01:06:33.670 --> 01:06:34.590
And then fold him up?

1558
01:06:34.650 --> 01:06:35.010
It sounds like it.

1559
01:06:35.170 --> 01:06:37.470
Then he folded him up and tried to shove him in a drum.

1560
01:06:37.830 --> 01:06:39.830
Teresa, that's exactly what it sounds like.

1561
01:06:40.170 --> 01:06:42.630
This is some of my favorite writing on our show.

1562
01:06:42.630 --> 01:06:47.030
In that moment in Casino Night, when Dwight just says he's wearing a tuxedo, my grandfather

1563
01:06:47.030 --> 01:06:47.850
was buried in it.

1564
01:06:47.970 --> 01:06:48.570
That's it.

1565
01:06:48.670 --> 01:06:49.790
We say no more about it.

1566
01:06:49.930 --> 01:06:54.190
And then three episodes later, he's like, when my grandfather was reburied.

1567
01:06:55.010 --> 01:06:57.750
And it's like, slowly, it's all coming together.

1568
01:06:58.570 --> 01:06:59.250
Sort of.

1569
01:06:59.430 --> 01:06:59.850
Sort of.

1570
01:06:59.910 --> 01:07:00.530
Not completely.

1571
01:07:01.030 --> 01:07:03.730
It's one of my favorite bits of how our show is written.

1572
01:07:04.090 --> 01:07:05.930
So now we're at reception, right?

1573
01:07:06.090 --> 01:07:09.530
And Pam has prepared this really beautiful box for the bird funeral.

1574
01:07:09.710 --> 01:07:11.230
I mean, it is amazing.

1575
01:07:11.230 --> 01:07:12.690
The attention to detail.

1576
01:07:13.130 --> 01:07:16.410
She asked Dwight if he would perhaps want to play a song.

1577
01:07:16.510 --> 01:07:17.690
She's like, do you have your recorder?

1578
01:07:17.830 --> 01:07:20.110
He's like, always, always has.

1579
01:07:20.450 --> 01:07:20.510
Always.

1580
01:07:20.510 --> 01:07:20.870
Always.

1581
01:07:22.330 --> 01:07:26.230
And, you know, Pam has this really sweet talking head where she's like, well, you know what?

1582
01:07:26.410 --> 01:07:30.610
I didn't anticipate I'd be throwing a bird funeral today, but you never know, working here.

1583
01:07:30.810 --> 01:07:31.430
That's right.

1584
01:07:31.550 --> 01:07:33.370
We had a fan question from Andrew S.

1585
01:07:33.470 --> 01:07:38.050
Did Jenna make the box for the bird funeral herself, or was that a Phil Shea creation?

1586
01:07:38.430 --> 01:07:40.970
Either way, how was it constructed?

1587
01:07:41.250 --> 01:07:42.950
Well, guys, I did not make it.

1588
01:07:43.250 --> 01:07:44.490
Phil Shea made it.

1589
01:07:44.490 --> 01:07:50.270
He said he actually made a few options and presented them again to Jen and Roger and Greg.

1590
01:07:50.470 --> 01:07:57.050
They picked the one that they liked, and then Phil had to make multiples of it because, you know, it gets set on fire.

1591
01:07:57.230 --> 01:07:57.450
Yeah.

1592
01:07:57.630 --> 01:07:59.290
So he had to make a whole bunch of those.

1593
01:07:59.670 --> 01:08:02.850
I looked it up in the script, and it was described in the script.

1594
01:08:02.850 --> 01:08:05.030
So here's what Phil had to go off of.

1595
01:08:05.190 --> 01:08:11.310
It said, it is a hollowed out tissue box with tissue padding in the center for the bird to lie on,

1596
01:08:11.350 --> 01:08:16.490
and the outside is decorated in tissue paper and has a tiny little toothpick cross.

1597
01:08:16.730 --> 01:08:18.870
My favorite part are the...

1598
01:08:18.870 --> 01:08:19.710
The handles.

1599
01:08:20.210 --> 01:08:21.090
The handles.

1600
01:08:21.850 --> 01:08:23.050
Made out of the pencils.

1601
01:08:23.050 --> 01:08:24.950
So that was a Phil addition.

1602
01:08:25.370 --> 01:08:26.870
That was just Phil.

1603
01:08:27.310 --> 01:08:27.510
Yeah.

1604
01:08:27.850 --> 01:08:28.090
Yeah.

1605
01:08:28.290 --> 01:08:34.750
He needed to make sure that tiny bird casket had little bars for the pallbearers.

1606
01:08:35.210 --> 01:08:36.010
That's right.

1607
01:08:36.270 --> 01:08:37.950
The tiny pallbearers.

1608
01:08:38.050 --> 01:08:38.470
Yes.

1609
01:08:38.670 --> 01:08:41.870
We are going to wrap things up in Stanford now, guys.

1610
01:08:42.150 --> 01:08:43.210
Jim found the chips.

1611
01:08:43.750 --> 01:08:45.230
He left them on her desk.

1612
01:08:45.350 --> 01:08:46.890
He's quite pleased with himself.

1613
01:08:47.230 --> 01:08:47.490
Yeah.

1614
01:08:47.490 --> 01:08:54.490
I really wanted my mom to record a sting that said, flirty Jim, but we couldn't get it together.

1615
01:08:54.950 --> 01:08:55.510
Aw.

1616
01:08:55.670 --> 01:08:57.370
Well, these scenes drove people crazy.

1617
01:08:57.790 --> 01:09:01.850
I went back and looked at some of the comments from when this aired.

1618
01:09:02.610 --> 01:09:03.170
Oh.

1619
01:09:03.470 --> 01:09:04.950
Here is what they said.

1620
01:09:05.890 --> 01:09:08.930
DTemp said, I feel kind of weird about Jim and Karen.

1621
01:09:09.790 --> 01:09:12.510
But Hartman said, the Karen thing?

1622
01:09:12.770 --> 01:09:14.650
Why am I liking it?

1623
01:09:14.730 --> 01:09:15.510
But I am.

1624
01:09:15.850 --> 01:09:16.130
Whoa.

1625
01:09:16.130 --> 01:09:19.290
Oh, I remember people freaking out about it.

1626
01:09:20.170 --> 01:09:23.550
Well, Keely said, Jim and Karen have great chemistry.

1627
01:09:24.250 --> 01:09:26.590
And Mandy said, okay, I'm sorry.

1628
01:09:26.730 --> 01:09:30.730
I can't believe there are actually people out there who are okay with Jim hooking up with Karen.

1629
01:09:30.930 --> 01:09:31.810
That's what I remember.

1630
01:09:32.890 --> 01:09:34.070
But Kim E.

1631
01:09:34.330 --> 01:09:35.070
said this.

1632
01:09:35.990 --> 01:09:36.330
Hmm.

1633
01:09:36.710 --> 01:09:39.030
Jim likes to buy chips for all his ladies.

1634
01:09:40.490 --> 01:09:42.050
And I'm with you, Kim.

1635
01:09:43.250 --> 01:09:46.130
As Pam, I'm like, oh, is this what you do?

1636
01:09:46.250 --> 01:09:46.470
Mm-hmm.

1637
01:09:46.730 --> 01:09:49.510
You just find all the girls their favorite chips?

1638
01:09:49.830 --> 01:09:50.010
Yeah.

1639
01:09:50.230 --> 01:09:51.090
That's his game.

1640
01:09:52.010 --> 01:09:53.190
Are we saying that right?

1641
01:09:53.450 --> 01:09:53.830
Oh.

1642
01:09:54.070 --> 01:09:54.790
Is that your game?

1643
01:09:54.790 --> 01:09:57.390
Jim and I, I 100% doubt we are.

1644
01:09:57.390 --> 01:09:58.930
Oh, Sam's giving us an okay.

1645
01:09:58.930 --> 01:09:59.730
Sam gave us an okay.

1646
01:09:59.910 --> 01:10:00.810
That's his game.

1647
01:10:01.570 --> 01:10:02.250
All right.

1648
01:10:02.390 --> 01:10:04.070
Should we go to this bird funeral, Angela?

1649
01:10:04.350 --> 01:10:05.230
I can't wait.

1650
01:10:05.710 --> 01:10:07.130
Please take me there.

1651
01:10:07.130 --> 01:10:12.070
Well, we're out in the driveway, really, next to the warehouse.

1652
01:10:12.450 --> 01:10:17.890
This was an actual parking lot that we used, and it was right next to the warehouse, and

1653
01:10:17.890 --> 01:10:19.030
that's where we filmed this.

1654
01:10:19.770 --> 01:10:23.010
Michael is very moved by Pam's beautiful coffin.

1655
01:10:23.390 --> 01:10:29.730
It starts with Pam saying a few words about the bird, but we slowly realize that Pam is speaking

1656
01:10:29.730 --> 01:10:32.350
about the bird, but she's speaking to Michael.

1657
01:10:32.570 --> 01:10:35.450
About his feelings about death and loss.

1658
01:10:35.450 --> 01:10:37.810
And about his aloneness.

1659
01:10:38.510 --> 01:10:45.270
She talks a lot about how just because the bird was alone doesn't mean he was unloved,

1660
01:10:45.470 --> 01:10:49.490
that he was very loved and respected by the other birds.

1661
01:10:49.750 --> 01:10:51.190
And then she sings a song.

1662
01:10:51.430 --> 01:10:53.670
I was very surprised by this for Pam.

1663
01:10:53.930 --> 01:10:55.450
She really put herself out there.

1664
01:10:55.570 --> 01:10:57.570
She sings On the Wings of Love.

1665
01:10:57.850 --> 01:11:00.130
She's accompanied by Dwight on the recorder.

1666
01:11:00.770 --> 01:11:02.230
Michael is very moved.

1667
01:11:02.350 --> 01:11:03.230
He's holding hands.

1668
01:11:03.230 --> 01:11:04.090
He's swaying.

1669
01:11:04.210 --> 01:11:05.130
He's tearing up.

1670
01:11:05.450 --> 01:11:07.710
We got a lot of questions about this scene.

1671
01:11:08.590 --> 01:11:09.430
Richard H.

1672
01:11:09.710 --> 01:11:10.750
really covers them all.

1673
01:11:11.030 --> 01:11:14.070
He says, did Rain already know how to play the recorder?

1674
01:11:14.470 --> 01:11:16.650
Did Jenna sing the entire song?

1675
01:11:16.810 --> 01:11:19.330
Or did the singing end where the scene was cut?

1676
01:11:19.930 --> 01:11:22.930
He also wanted to know, did we rehearse our duet?

1677
01:11:23.710 --> 01:11:25.070
Here's what I can tell you.

1678
01:11:26.150 --> 01:11:28.250
Yes, Rain knew how to play the recorder.

1679
01:11:28.990 --> 01:11:31.190
Remember, he played it in Take Your Daughter to Work Day.

1680
01:11:31.190 --> 01:11:32.730
He was actually very good.

1681
01:11:33.010 --> 01:11:36.770
But listen to all of the instruments that Rain can play in real life, Angela.

1682
01:11:37.390 --> 01:11:48.890
He can play the recorder, piano, clarinet, saxophone, bassoon, guitar, drums, xylophone, bells, and a little tuba and baritone.

1683
01:11:48.890 --> 01:11:50.970
What are you, a one-man band, Rain?

1684
01:11:51.410 --> 01:11:52.510
He kind of is.

1685
01:11:52.710 --> 01:11:56.470
I knew you could play the bassoon because your book, The Bassoon King, is great.

1686
01:11:56.470 --> 01:11:57.610
So I knew that.

1687
01:11:58.010 --> 01:12:00.790
But I didn't know you were a walking one-man show.

1688
01:12:01.090 --> 01:12:03.550
Suffice it to say, Rain knew his stuff.

1689
01:12:04.250 --> 01:12:08.650
And we did rehearse, but just a little bit right before the scene.

1690
01:12:08.870 --> 01:12:10.430
Rain and I practiced separately.

1691
01:12:10.430 --> 01:12:13.790
I had listened to the song over and over again to memorize the words.

1692
01:12:13.950 --> 01:12:17.430
I had to sing the first verse through the chorus.

1693
01:12:18.190 --> 01:12:20.110
That's what I had to do over and over again.

1694
01:12:20.730 --> 01:12:22.190
I was so nervous.

1695
01:12:22.970 --> 01:12:24.350
Well, I thought you did fantastic.

1696
01:12:25.450 --> 01:12:26.230
Thank you.

1697
01:12:26.470 --> 01:12:28.670
I have a fan question from the scene, Jenna.

1698
01:12:29.430 --> 01:12:29.730
Okay.

1699
01:12:30.250 --> 01:12:35.470
So Chelsea wrote in and said, was Angela's A Man improvised?

1700
01:12:35.810 --> 01:12:40.070
A lot of people wrote in about this because it was so quick after Pam was done talking.

1701
01:12:40.070 --> 01:12:42.130
People wanted to know if I improvised that.

1702
01:12:42.330 --> 01:12:43.870
No, that was scripted.

1703
01:12:44.350 --> 01:12:47.530
And folks wanted to know, why did Angela do it?

1704
01:12:47.610 --> 01:12:54.270
And I said, I think personally, the direction that they gave me was that Angela had had enough

1705
01:12:54.270 --> 01:12:58.130
of this charade, this bird funeral.

1706
01:12:58.330 --> 01:12:59.730
Pam had gone on and on.

1707
01:12:59.830 --> 01:13:00.090
Enough.

1708
01:13:00.230 --> 01:13:00.490
Amen.

1709
01:13:00.670 --> 01:13:01.410
I'm done.

1710
01:13:01.770 --> 01:13:03.990
And that's how you finish it.

1711
01:13:04.070 --> 01:13:04.430
Yes.

1712
01:13:04.710 --> 01:13:04.950
Amen.

1713
01:13:04.950 --> 01:13:05.270
Amen.

1714
01:13:05.410 --> 01:13:05.850
I'm finished.

1715
01:13:06.070 --> 01:13:06.210
Amen.

1716
01:13:06.430 --> 01:13:06.970
Stop talking.

1717
01:13:07.750 --> 01:13:08.710
Yes, exactly.

1718
01:13:09.570 --> 01:13:11.670
Speaking of the script, I have a script catch.

1719
01:13:12.030 --> 01:13:14.390
So, you know, in the scene, we set the box on fire.

1720
01:13:14.630 --> 01:13:17.590
In the shooting draft of the script, we buried the bird.

1721
01:13:18.310 --> 01:13:22.890
There was a scene where Michael and Dwight are digging a hole outside, and then we all

1722
01:13:22.890 --> 01:13:24.050
gather around this hole.

1723
01:13:24.550 --> 01:13:27.390
And it got changed to fire, but I don't know why.

1724
01:13:27.390 --> 01:13:33.870
I think maybe, Angela, there was really no grass in this area that we shot.

1725
01:13:33.870 --> 01:13:34.750
We had no grass.

1726
01:13:34.930 --> 01:13:39.830
There was a hedge with maybe, like, I don't know, three feet of dirt.

1727
01:13:40.230 --> 01:13:42.630
Like, yeah, there was, I mean, not even.

1728
01:13:42.850 --> 01:13:47.630
There is a deleted scene where Michael and Dwight are trying to dig a hole there, and

1729
01:13:47.630 --> 01:13:50.310
then the business on the other side yells at them, and they run away.

1730
01:13:51.090 --> 01:13:52.390
Oh, okay.

1731
01:13:52.750 --> 01:13:53.610
Well, there you go.

1732
01:13:53.610 --> 01:13:57.390
Well, we had another fan question about this fire, then.

1733
01:13:57.770 --> 01:14:01.590
Clara Johnson wants to know, did you actually set the bird on fire?

1734
01:14:02.450 --> 01:14:02.970
Yes.

1735
01:14:03.150 --> 01:14:04.710
Real fire, fake bird.

1736
01:14:04.970 --> 01:14:10.110
Kent told me they had to hire a special effects guy to oversee the fire and the extinguishing,

1737
01:14:10.110 --> 01:14:13.570
and they had to get a special permit to be allowed to set the fire.

1738
01:14:13.750 --> 01:14:15.830
And he said we did three takes.

1739
01:14:16.450 --> 01:14:17.670
Three takes of the fire.

1740
01:14:17.670 --> 01:14:22.950
And Phil told me, though, that the special effects guys were worried that the fake dead

1741
01:14:22.950 --> 01:14:25.990
bird might give off toxic fumes when it burned.

1742
01:14:26.030 --> 01:14:30.630
So they actually took it out of the box and just put a wad of toilet paper in there.

1743
01:14:30.990 --> 01:14:32.930
Oh, not toilet paper.

1744
01:14:33.770 --> 01:14:34.750
I know.

1745
01:14:35.450 --> 01:14:36.030
Too soon.

1746
01:14:36.130 --> 01:14:36.180
I know.

1747
01:14:36.370 --> 01:14:37.190
Too soon, Angela.

1748
01:14:37.490 --> 01:14:37.970
Too soon.

1749
01:14:39.530 --> 01:14:43.950
And then finally, we had a fan catch from Peter K.

1750
01:14:43.950 --> 01:14:47.990
He said, I noticed the box was burned in some shredded paper.

1751
01:14:48.330 --> 01:14:51.870
Could this be further evidence that Pam does use the shredder?

1752
01:14:52.270 --> 01:14:53.930
Oh, look at that.

1753
01:14:54.390 --> 01:14:55.410
Nice one, Peter.

1754
01:14:55.750 --> 01:14:56.890
It would appear so.

1755
01:14:57.290 --> 01:15:01.850
Well, you know, Jen actually left us a really great message about this scene.

1756
01:15:02.230 --> 01:15:07.170
And she was talking about the fact that we never got to shoot outside.

1757
01:15:07.170 --> 01:15:10.170
And when we did, everyone would get really excited.

1758
01:15:10.490 --> 01:15:12.790
And she explains a little bit about that.

1759
01:15:12.790 --> 01:15:14.430
So, Sam, can you play that?

1760
01:15:14.970 --> 01:15:20.890
One thing that I remember from the grief counseling episode is that when I went to the sound mix,

1761
01:15:21.150 --> 01:15:23.470
they were showing us the playback of the episode.

1762
01:15:24.030 --> 01:15:27.270
And it was still a little bit unusual when the characters went outside.

